.TH DANBYVIZTEST 1
.SH NAME
danbyviztest \- check that the danby models' pictures make sense
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B danbyviztest
[
.B -v
] [
.I model
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IR Danbylabtest (1)
checks the equations.  This checks the DRAWING, which is a different
thing and can be wrong while every number is right.
.PP
Nothing here opens a window.  That is the point: the geometry of a
picture is arithmetic, and arithmetic can be checked where there is no
display.  Each model is run, and each of its points is put through the
same mapping
.IR danbylab (2)'s
own
.B drawmechanism()
uses, so what is measured is where the thing would actually be drawn.
.SH "THE CHECK THAT MATTERS"
.IR Danbylab (2)
scales a mechanism's diagram to the bounding box of the model's points.
If the only points are the moving ones, the view is scaled to the very
thing it is drawing \(em and that thing then never appears to move.
.PP
.IR Danby-schrodinger (1)
shipped like that: its marker sat at 0.885 of the pane's width from a
fifth of the way through the run to the end, because the box's right
edge WAS the marker.  Every number in it was correct.
.PP
Eleven models had it, found by this and fixed: duffing, van der Pol,
variationparameters, movingpivot, nonlinearsprings and
carbonmicrophone all had a wall pinning one side of the view and
nothing pinning the other; joggingcompanion had the leader pinning one
end and the follower BEING the other; fireflies had nothing in the
picture but the four flies; the two rockets rode up with their own
nose and fins and had no ground to leave; synchronousmotor had a stator
with east, west and south poles and no north.
.PP
The fix in every case is a fixed point at the extreme the motion was
defining, declared
.B plain
so it pins the frame and draws nothing.
.SH "WHAT IS FAILED AND WHAT IS NOTED"
A point that DEFINES an extreme of the box necessarily sits at the same
place on screen \(em the topmost thing is always at the top \(em and
that is not a defect by itself.  A rocket whose nose is the highest
point still reads as flying, because its base and fins cross the pane.
.PP
So the judgement is made over the whole picture: it fails when NOT ONE
moving point gets anywhere on screen.  A point that is merely
compressed is noted, as is a rocket whose view follows it once it is
up \(em which cannot be otherwise, since these climb to about 1500
units and are 1.4 long, and a frame holding the whole flight would draw
the rocket a thousandth of a pane high.
.PP
A profile whose density reaches zero is noted rather than failed: that
is the surface of the star, and the march holding it there is the right
answer.
.IR Danby-starmodel (1)
says so itself, with an
.B "eq live step { density }"
that stops the derivative at the same place.
.SH "KNOWN, AND NOT A SLIP"
.B Tablependulum
goes not-a-number at t = 0.56.  It is real: the bob is drawn towards the
pivot, the pendulum length hits its floor, and omega runs away with the
angular momentum exactly as the equations say it should.  Ending that
properly means modelling the string reaching its end, which is a
decision about the physics rather than a guard to bolt on, so it is
recorded here and not quietly patched.  Anything else going
not-a-number is new, and fails.
.SH BUGS
It looks at the first 120 frames, which at the mechanism family's own
step is 2.4 seconds of model time.  A model whose picture is fine at the
start and degenerates later will pass.
.PP
Only the mechanism family's diagram is checked this way.  Orbit has its
own focus-centred extent and population its bars, and neither is
measured here; the four families with no diagram at all are only checked
for their observables.
.PP
It cannot tell whether a picture is a good picture.  It can tell whether
the thing that moves appears to.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/danbyviztest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR danbylabtest (1),
.IR danbylab (2),
.IR danby (1)
